export const ARTICLES_BATCH4: Article[] = [
  {
    slug: 'womens-world-cup-history',
    category: 'history',
    title: 'The Women’s World Cup: A Short History of Football’s Other Great Tournament',
    excerpt:
      'From a cautious twelve-team experiment in China in 1991 to a 32-team event across two countries in 2023, the Women’s World Cup has become one of sport’s great stages. How it began, the teams and players who shaped it, and where it goes next.',
    date: '2026-10-13',
    minutes: 9,
    author: 'The Xaa Desk',
    sections: [
      { h: '', p: [
        'Every four years the men’s World Cup takes over the planet, but it is not football’s only world championship. The FIFA Women’s World Cup, first played in 1991, has grown from a cautious twelve-team experiment into one of the largest events in women’s sport, with crowds, television audiences and commercial interest that would have seemed unimaginable to the players who started it.',
        'Its story is shorter than the men’s tournament, but it is crowded with drama: a penalty shootout in front of a record crowd, a team that won the trophy without conceding a goal, a champion that lifted a grieving nation and a final effectively settled by a hat-trick inside sixteen minutes. Here is how the tournament began, the teams and players who shaped it and where it is heading.'] },
      { h: 'Before FIFA: a game pushed to the margins', p: [
        'Women have played organised football for almost as long as men, and at times they drew enormous crowds. In England during and just after the First World War, factory teams such as Dick, Kerr Ladies of Preston became genuine attractions; on Boxing Day 1920 their match against St Helens Ladies at Goodison Park in Liverpool drew a crowd of around 53,000.',
        'The response of the authorities was not to celebrate that popularity but to shut it down. In 1921 the Football Association banned women’s teams from the grounds of its member clubs, declaring that the game was “quite unsuitable for females”. The ban was not lifted until 1971, and similar restrictions held the women’s game back in several other countries for decades.',
        'Even so, the idea of an international tournament would not go away. Unofficial world championships were staged in Italy in 1970 and in Mexico in 1971, the second played in large stadiums including the Estadio Azteca, and Denmark won both. They proved that there was an audience. It took FIFA another two decades to act on it.'] },
      { h: 'A test event, then the first World Cup', p: [
        'In 1988 FIFA organised an invitational women’s tournament in China as a trial. Twelve teams took part and Norway beat Sweden in the final. The event was well attended and well played, and it persuaded FIFA that a full championship could succeed.',
        'The first official tournament followed in China in 1991, again with twelve teams. It was not even called the World Cup at the time, and its matches lasted eighty minutes instead of ninety — a sign of how cautious the organisers still were. The United States won it, beating Norway 2–1 in the final in Guangzhou, with Michelle Akers scoring both goals. Akers finished as the tournament’s top scorer, and the American team that won that day became the foundation of the most successful national side in the women’s game.'] },
      { h: 'Norway’s title and a summer at the Rose Bowl', p: [
        'Sweden hosted the second tournament in 1995, now played over the full ninety minutes, and Norway — beaten finalists four years earlier — won it, defeating Germany 2–0 in the final in Solna. Their direct, athletic style and organised defending made them the dominant European side of the era.',
        'Then came 1999. The United States hosted a sixteen-team tournament in large stadiums, and it became a national event. The final against China at the Rose Bowl in Pasadena was watched by 90,185 spectators, at the time a record for a women’s sporting event. After a goalless 120 minutes, goalkeeper Briana Scurry saved a Chinese penalty and Brandi Chastain struck the winning kick.',
        'That afternoon changed the perception of the women’s game in the United States and far beyond. It showed broadcasters and sponsors that a women’s football tournament could fill the biggest stadiums, and it inspired a generation of girls to take up the sport.'] },
      { h: 'Germany’s decade', p: [
        'The 2003 tournament had been awarded to China, but an outbreak of the SARS virus led FIFA to move it to the United States at short notice. Germany won it, beating Sweden 2–1 in the final with a golden goal in extra time from Nia Künzer — the only time a Women’s World Cup final has been decided that way, as the golden-goal rule was abandoned soon afterwards.',
        'Four years later in China, Germany did something no team had done before: they won the tournament without conceding a single goal. In the final in Shanghai they beat Brazil 2–0, and goalkeeper Nadine Angerer saved a penalty from Marta, the Brazilian forward who was named the tournament’s best player and finished as its top scorer. Germany became the first nation to retain the women’s title.',
        'That German side was built on depth and discipline, and on Birgit Prinz, a powerful forward who for years held the record as the tournament’s all-time leading scorer.'] },
      { h: 'Japan 2011: a title for a grieving country', p: [
        'The 2011 tournament in Germany produced one of the most moving champions in sport. Japan arrived only months after the earthquake and tsunami of March 2011 had devastated the north-east of the country. Their patient, technical passing game carried them past the hosts in the quarter-finals and into a final against the United States in Frankfurt.',
        'Twice the Americans led and twice Japan came back, the second time through a late extra-time goal from their captain Homare Sawa. Japan then won the penalty shootout and became the first Asian nation to win a World Cup. Sawa was named the best player and finished as top scorer, the reward for a career that had already taken her to every tournament since 1995.'] },
      { h: 'Bigger tournaments, new arguments', p: [
        'Canada hosted the first 24-team tournament in 2015. It was the first senior World Cup played entirely on artificial turf, a decision that a group of leading players challenged in a legal complaint and that many still regard as one the men’s game would never have accepted. The football itself was memorable: in the final in Vancouver, the United States’ Carli Lloyd scored a hat-trick inside the first sixteen minutes against Japan, the third struck from the halfway line, and the Americans won 5–2.',
        'France 2019 brought the video assistant referee to the women’s tournament for the first time, large television audiences across Europe and another American title. The United States beat the Netherlands 2–0 in the final in Lyon, with Megan Rapinoe — named best player and top scorer — converting a penalty and Rose Lavelle adding a second. The win made them only the second team, after Germany, to retain the trophy.'] },
      { h: 'Two hosts, thirty-two teams and a first-time champion', p: [
        'The 2023 tournament was the first to be co-hosted, by Australia and New Zealand, and the first with 32 teams. The expansion rewarded several nations outside the traditional elite: Morocco, Jamaica and South Africa all reached the knockout rounds, while co-hosts Australia’s run to the semi-finals produced some of the largest television audiences in that country’s history.',
        'The final in Sydney matched Spain against England, the reigning European champions. Spain won 1–0 with a goal from left-back Olga Carmona, adding the senior world title to the youth world titles their system had already been collecting. Their midfielder Aitana Bonmatí was named the best player of the tournament, and Spain became only the fifth nation to be crowned women’s world champion, after the United States, Norway, Germany and Japan.'] },
      { h: 'The players who defined it', p: [
        'Every tournament has its stars, but a few players have shaped the Women’s World Cup across many editions. Brazil’s Marta is its all-time leading scorer with 17 goals, scored across five tournaments, and she played at six World Cups in total. Her compatriot Formiga, a tireless midfielder, appeared at seven, from 1995 to 2019 — more than any player, man or woman, in World Cup history.',
        'Germany’s Birgit Prinz, the United States’ Mia Hamm and Abby Wambach, Norway’s Hege Riise, China’s Sun Wen and Canada’s Christine Sinclair all left their mark, as did goalkeepers such as Hope Solo and Nadine Angerer. Many of them spent much of their careers with far fewer resources than their male counterparts, and their success helped to build the professional leagues in which today’s players compete.'] },
      { h: 'The same game, a different journey', p: [
        'On the pitch, the women’s tournament is played under exactly the same Laws of the Game as the men’s. The differences lie elsewhere: in the history of investment, in how recently professional leagues have developed in many countries and in the depth of youth systems. Those gaps help to explain why the early tournaments were dominated by a handful of nations that took the women’s game seriously first, among them the United States, Norway, Germany and China.',
        'As more federations invest, the map is widening. Spain and England, relative latecomers to the very top, met in the 2023 final; teams from Africa, Asia, Central America and the Caribbean have become more competitive; and the gap between the strongest teams and the rest, while still real, has narrowed with each edition.'] },
      { h: 'What comes next', p: [
        'The 2027 tournament will be played in Brazil, the first time the Women’s World Cup has been held in South America. Beyond that, the direction is clear: bigger audiences, more professional players and more countries that believe they can compete for the trophy.',
        'For anyone who follows the men’s World Cup, the women’s tournament deserves the same attention. It has its own rivalries, its own legends and its own style, and its history is still being written at speed.'] },
      { h: 'In short', p: [
        'The Women’s World Cup began in China in 1991 after decades in which the women’s game was restricted or ignored, and it has expanded from twelve teams to thirty-two. Five nations have won it — the United States, Norway, Germany, Japan and Spain — and its history includes some of football’s most memorable finals.',
        'Its future looks bigger still, with Brazil hosting in 2027. Whether you come to it from the men’s game or watch it for its own sake, it is a tournament with a story worth knowing.'] },
    ],
  },
  {
    slug: 'how-footballers-choose-national-team',
    category: 'teams',
    title: 'Born Here, Playing There: How Footballers Choose Their National Team',
    excerpt:
      'Many World Cup players could have represented more than one country. How FIFA’s eligibility rules work — nationality, birth, family and residence — when a player becomes tied to one nation, the narrow route to a single switch, and why the choice is rarely simple.',
    date: '2026-10-13',
    minutes: 9,
    author: 'The Xaa Desk',
    sections: [
      { h: '', p: [
        'Look closely at almost any World Cup squad and you will find players who could have worn another shirt: a forward born in one country to parents from another, a defender who grew up abroad, a midfielder who represented one nation as a teenager and another as an adult. Modern football is full of players with more than one footballing home, and the choice between them can shape careers and even tournaments.',
        'The rules that govern those choices are set by FIFA, and they are stricter than many fans assume. This guide explains how a player qualifies to represent a country, when that choice becomes permanent, the narrow route that allows one change, and the very human reasons behind the decisions players make.'] },
      { h: 'Nationality comes first', p: [
        'The starting point is simple: to play for a national team, a player must hold the nationality of that country. Football does not create citizenship; it relies on each state’s own nationality laws, which vary enormously. Some countries grant citizenship to anyone born on their territory, others pass it through parents or grandparents, and some allow it after a period of residence.',
        'Holding a passport is necessary, but it is not always enough. FIFA adds its own conditions to stop countries from simply naturalising talented foreign players to strengthen their teams. Those conditions are designed to ensure a genuine connection between the player and the country he or she represents.'] },
      { h: 'The routes to eligibility', p: [
        'A player who holds more than one nationality, or who acquires a new one, must also show that connection. The usual routes are being born in the country, or having a parent or grandparent who was born there. These cover most international footballers with more than one option: children of migrants, players whose families moved when they were young and those with a grandparent from another country.',
        'A player who moved to a country as an adult and acquired its nationality there, without any of those family links, must have lived there continuously for at least five years after turning eighteen. Earlier rules required only two years. The tightening followed several attempts in the early 2000s to naturalise established players who had never lived in the country they would represent — exactly the kind of transfer between nations that the rules are meant to prevent.'] },
      { h: 'Shared passports and special cases', p: [
        'Some situations need extra rules. The four British associations — England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland — all represent people who hold the same British passport, so they have agreed criteria, based mainly on birth and family, to decide who can play for which. Similar questions arise for territories that have their own football association but share a nationality with a larger state.',
        'There are also special provisions for players whose nationality changed through no choice of their own, for example when a country broke apart and new states were created. Players affected by such changes have been able to represent a new country even after playing for the old one.'] },
      { h: 'When a choice becomes permanent', p: [
        'For most players, the decisive moment is the first appearance for a senior national team in an official competition: a World Cup or continental championship match, or a qualifier for one. Once a player has taken part in such a match, he or she is generally tied to that country for good. Youth internationals do not tie a player, and nor do senior friendlies.',
        'This creates a well-known tactic. When a promising dual national emerges, a national coach may select the player for a competitive match — sometimes as a late substitute in a qualifier — partly to secure his or her future. Fans call it cap-tying, and it has decided the international careers of more than a few players.',
        'Because friendlies do not count, some players have changed countries even after playing for a senior team. Diego Costa played two friendlies for Brazil in 2013 before choosing Spain; Wilfried Zaha appeared in friendlies for England before switching to Ivory Coast; and Declan Rice played three friendlies for the Republic of Ireland before committing to England in 2019.'] },
      { h: 'The one-time switch', p: [
        'Since 2020, FIFA’s rules have allowed a single change of association even after a competitive senior appearance, but only under tight conditions. Broadly, the player must already have held the new nationality when first playing an official match for the original country; must have played no more than three senior matches for it; must have been no older than 21 at the time of the last official match for it; must have waited at least three years since the last senior appearance; and must never have played at a World Cup or continental championship finals for it.',
        'The change was designed for players who were capped young, often briefly, and then found themselves locked out of international football. One of the first to benefit was Munir El Haddadi, who had made a single competitive appearance for Spain as a teenager in 2014 and later went on to play for Morocco. The switch is allowed once only: a player who changes cannot change back.'] },
      { h: 'How players actually decide', p: [
        'The regulations decide what is possible; the decision itself is personal. Players weigh identity and family — the country their parents left, the community they grew up in, the language spoken at home — alongside very practical questions. Which team offers a realistic chance of playing regularly? Which is more likely to qualify for major tournaments? Which coach has shown faith in them, and how much competition is there for their position?',
        'Timing matters too. A young player who is overlooked by one federation may be warmly courted by another, and an early call-up can create a bond that lasts a career. Some players describe the choice as obvious from childhood; others agonise for years. Public reaction can be harsh in both directions, which is one reason many players keep their options quiet until the last moment.'] },
      { h: 'Brothers on opposite sides', p: [
        'Few stories show the reality of dual eligibility as clearly as that of the Boateng half-brothers. Both were born in Berlin to a Ghanaian father. Jérôme Boateng chose Germany, while Kevin-Prince Boateng, who had played for Germany’s youth teams, chose Ghana. The two met on opposite sides at the 2010 World Cup in South Africa and again in 2014 in Brazil — the first time brothers had faced each other at a World Cup.',
        'Their story is not unusual in modern football. Morocco’s run to the semi-finals in 2022, the first by an African team at a World Cup, was built partly on players born and raised in Europe, among them Achraf Hakimi, born in Madrid, and Hakim Ziyech and Sofyan Amrabat, both born in the Netherlands. Many of the strongest teams in the world draw on the children and grandchildren of migrants, and many smaller nations rely on their diaspora to compete at all.'] },
      { h: 'A history of moving between flags', p: [
        'In football’s early decades the rules were looser still. Luis Monti played in the 1930 World Cup final for Argentina and the 1934 final for Italy, the only man to appear in finals for two different countries. Ferenc Puskás starred for Hungary and later played for Spain, and Alfredo Di Stéfano represented Argentina before playing for Spain. Later rules closed that door for players who had already appeared in competitive matches.',
        'The break-up of states created another kind of switch. Robert Prosinečki played for Yugoslavia at the 1990 World Cup and for Croatia in 1998, scoring at both tournaments — a rare player to have scored World Cup goals for two different countries.'] },
      { h: 'Why it matters in the 48-team era', p: [
        'The expanded World Cup raises the stakes. With more places available, more countries have a realistic chance of qualifying, and that makes them more attractive to players with a choice. Federations now scout abroad systematically, tracking eligible players in foreign leagues and academies, and the competition to persuade a talented teenager can begin years before a senior debut.',
        'For fans, it is worth remembering that a player who represents a parent’s or grandparent’s homeland is not a mercenary. The rules require a genuine link, and for many players the choice reflects who they are. For the teams, the challenge is to turn players from different footballing cultures into a side that plays with a shared identity — something the best-run federations treat as seriously as recruitment itself.'] },
      { h: 'Common misunderstandings', p: [
        '“He was born there, so he has to play for them.” Not so: birth creates eligibility, not an obligation. “She played for their under-21s, so she is tied.” Youth matches do not tie a player. “He played a friendly, so he can never switch.” Senior friendlies do not tie a player either.',
        '“He can switch whenever he likes.” He cannot. Once a player is tied by a competitive senior appearance, only the narrow one-time route described above remains, and many players do not meet its conditions. And “anyone with a passport can play”: a passport is necessary, but without a genuine connection — birth, a parent or grandparent, or long residence — it is not enough.'] },
      { h: 'In short', p: [
        'A footballer can represent a country only if he or she holds its nationality and, where the rules require it, has a genuine connection to it through birth, a parent or grandparent born there, or a long period of residence. A competitive senior appearance usually ties a player for good; youth matches and friendlies do not.',
        'Since 2020, players capped briefly and young may switch once under strict conditions. Within those rules the decision is personal — a mix of identity, family and ambition — and at a World Cup full of dual nationals it is part of what makes the tournament such a vivid picture of the modern world.'] },
    ],
  },
  {
    slug: 'world-cup-awards-golden-boot-ball-glove',
    category: 'players',
    title: 'Golden Boot, Golden Ball, Golden Glove: How the World Cup’s Individual Awards Work',
    excerpt:
      'Football is a team game, but every World Cup ends by naming its top scorer, best player, finest goalkeeper and outstanding young talent. How each award is decided, how ties are broken, and what the winners’ lists reveal about the tournament.',
    date: '2026-10-13',
    minutes: 9,
    author: 'The Xaa Desk',
    sections: [
      { h: '', p: [
        'Football is the ultimate team game, and the World Cup is decided by teams. Yet every tournament also ends with a small ceremony for individuals: the top scorer, the best player, the finest goalkeeper and the most impressive young talent. The winners are remembered alongside the champions, and sometimes instead of them.',
        'The awards are simple in principle but have rules and quirks that are worth knowing — how ties are broken, who decides and why the best player of a World Cup so often ends up on the losing side of the final. This guide explains each award and what its history tells us.'] },
      { h: 'The Golden Boot: goals first, then the tie-breakers', p: [
        'The Golden Boot goes to the tournament’s top scorer. Goals scored in normal time and in extra time count, including penalties awarded during play; goals in a penalty shootout do not, because a shootout is a way of deciding a drawn tie rather than part of the match itself.',
        'Because several players often finish level, FIFA uses tie-breakers. If two or more players have scored the same number of goals, the award goes to the one with more assists; if they are still level, it goes to the player who spent fewer minutes on the pitch. In 2010 four players finished with five goals each, and Germany’s Thomas Müller took the award on assists.',
        'The runners-up receive the Silver and Bronze Boots. The award has carried different names and sponsors over the years, but the idea is older than any trophy: the leading scorer of every tournament since 1930 is part of the record books, even though the formal award only began in 1982.'] },
      { h: 'Scoring records', p: [
        'The record for goals at a single World Cup belongs to France’s Just Fontaine, who scored 13 in 1958 — an astonishing total from six matches that survived every tournament up to and including 2022. West Germany’s Gerd Müller scored 10 in 1970, and in 2002 Brazil’s Ronaldo scored eight, including both goals in the final.',
        'Across all tournaments, the leading scorer at the end of 2022 was Germany’s Miroslav Klose, with 16 goals over four World Cups, one ahead of Ronaldo. In 2022 Kylian Mbappé became only the second man to score a hat-trick in a World Cup final, after England’s Geoff Hurst in 1966, and won the Golden Boot with eight goals.'] },
      { h: 'Assists: the statistic behind the tie-break', p: [
        'Assists decide Golden Boot ties, but they are less straightforward than goals. An assist is generally the final pass or touch that leads directly to a goal, yet deciding whether a deflected pass, a blocked shot that falls kindly or a won penalty should count involves judgement, and different data providers have used slightly different definitions over the years.',
        'In practice the tie-breakers are rarely needed for the very best scorers: in most recent tournaments the Golden Boot winner has finished clear on goals. When the margin is narrow, though, it is worth remembering that the second criterion rests on a more subjective statistic than the first.'] },
      { h: 'The Golden Ball: the best player', p: [
        'The Golden Ball honours the best player of the tournament. It was first awarded in 1982, when Italy’s Paolo Rossi completed an extraordinary summer by winning the World Cup, the Golden Ball and the top-scorer award. In the modern era FIFA’s technical study group — a panel of former coaches and players who analyse every match — plays the central role in the selection, and the runners-up receive the Silver and Bronze Balls.',
        'The list of winners reads like a history of the game’s great players: Diego Maradona in 1986, Romário in 1994, Ronaldo in 1998, Zinedine Zidane in 2006 and Luka Modrić in 2018. Lionel Messi won it in 2014 and again in 2022, the first player to win it twice. Germany’s Oliver Kahn, in 2002, became the first goalkeeper to receive it.'] },
      { h: 'What the Golden Ball list reveals', p: [
        'Two patterns stand out. The first is that the award almost always goes to an attacker or a creative midfielder: from 1982 to 2022, no outfield defender won it. Defending well is often a matter of what does not happen — a pass intercepted, a run tracked, a chance prevented — and that is harder to notice and to celebrate than a goal or a dribble.',
        'The second is that the best player is frequently on the losing side. Ronaldo in 1998, Kahn in 2002, Zidane in 2006, Messi in 2014 and Modrić in 2018 all received the Golden Ball after losing the final, and Diego Forlán won it in 2010 for a Uruguay team that finished fourth. Individual brilliance can carry a team a long way, but winning a World Cup usually needs more than one outstanding player.'] },
      { h: 'The Golden Glove', p: [
        'The award for the best goalkeeper was introduced in 1994 as the Lev Yashin Award, named after the great Soviet goalkeeper, and has been called the Golden Glove since 2010. Its first winner was Belgium’s Michel Preud’homme. Later winners include Fabien Barthez, Gianluigi Buffon, Iker Casillas, Manuel Neuer, Thibaut Courtois and, in 2022, Argentina’s Emiliano Martínez, whose save in the last moments of extra time against France kept his team alive in the final.',
        'Judging goalkeepers is notoriously difficult. A goalkeeper on a dominant team may face few shots, and a single error can overshadow a run of excellent matches. Modern analysis helps by comparing the quality of shots faced with the goals actually conceded, but the award still relies heavily on judgement — and penalty shootouts, which are not part of the match itself, often shape the way a goalkeeper’s tournament is remembered.'] },
      { h: 'The Best Young Player award', p: [
        'Since 2006 FIFA has also named the best young player of the tournament, an award open to players aged 21 or under at the start of the tournament year. Germany’s Lukas Podolski won the first. Thomas Müller won it in 2010, Paul Pogba in 2014, Kylian Mbappé in 2018 and Argentina’s Enzo Fernández in 2022.',
        'The award has a good record of identifying future stars, which is fitting: the World Cup has always been a stage on which young players announce themselves, from Pelé as a 17-year-old in 1958 to Michael Owen’s solo goal against Argentina in 1998.'] },
      { h: 'Fair play and player of the match', p: [
        'FIFA also presents a Fair Play Trophy to the team with the best disciplinary record and names a player of the match after every game. These awards attract less attention, but they recognise something important: the tournament is meant to be played in a certain spirit, and every match has individuals who make the difference.',
        'The player-of-the-match award in particular has become a fixture of every broadcast, and it often goes to a goalscorer in the winning team. That is understandable, but it is worth asking after each game whether someone else — a defender who cleared off the line, a midfielder who quietly controlled the tempo — had the stronger claim.'] },
      { h: 'Why individual awards are hard to judge', p: [
        'A World Cup is a short competition. In the 32-team format even the finalists played only seven matches, against opponents of very different strength. A player can score a hat-trick against a weak side and look like the tournament’s best finisher, or play superbly in a team that is eliminated early and be forgotten. The sample is small, and context matters enormously.',
        'Roles matter too. A centre-forward is judged on goals, a playmaker on chances created and a holding midfielder on things that rarely make the highlights. The best way to read the awards is as a summary of who shaped the tournament most visibly, not as a definitive ranking of who played best.'] },
      { h: 'How the 48-team format changes the maths', p: [
        'The expanded tournament adds a round of 32, so teams that reach the final now play eight matches instead of seven. More matches mean more chances to score and more opportunities to impress, and the group stage brings more meetings between the strongest teams and the newest qualifiers. Comparisons with the past will need care: a total reached in a 104-match World Cup is not directly comparable with one achieved in the smaller tournaments of earlier eras.',
        'The same applies to other totals. A team that plays more matches has more chances to collect assists, saves and clean sheets, so figures per match, or per ninety minutes, will become more useful than raw totals when comparing players across different eras of the tournament.'] },
      { h: 'Picking your own winners', p: [
        'If you want to judge the awards yourself, look beyond the highlight reels. For forwards, consider the quality of their chances as well as their goals; for creators, how many good chances they set up; for defenders and holding midfielders, how often attacks broke down around them. For goalkeepers, ask whether they kept their team in matches it might have lost, not just how many clean sheets they collected.',
        'Above all, notice who performed in the matches that mattered most. A goal in a group game against a much weaker side counts the same in the scoring charts as a goal in a semi-final, but most fans — and most juries — remember the second far longer.'] },
      { h: 'In short', p: [
        'The Golden Boot goes to the top scorer, with assists and then minutes played as tie-breakers, and shootout goals do not count. The Golden Ball goes to the best player and has often been won by someone on the losing side; the Golden Glove honours the best goalkeeper; and the Best Young Player award has a strong record of spotting future greats.',
        'Enjoy the awards as part of the tournament’s story, but remember that the World Cup itself is won by teams — and that some of the players who matter most rarely appear on the podium.'] },
    ],
  },
  {
    slug: 'football-or-soccer-name-history',
    category: 'culture',
    title: 'Football or Soccer? The Surprising Story Behind the Game’s Two Names',
    excerpt:
      '“Soccer” sounds American, but the word was born in England. How the game got its two English names, why North America kept one and Britain dropped it, and what the sport is called in languages around the world.',
    date: '2026-10-13',
    minutes: 9,
    author: 'The Xaa Desk',
    sections: [
      { h: '', p: [
        'At a World Cup shared between the United States, Canada and Mexico, the game goes by different names depending on where you are standing. In Mexico it is fútbol; in most of Canada and the United States it is soccer; to most of the rest of the world it is football. The variety causes plenty of friendly arguments, and the word “soccer” is often mocked as an Americanism.',
        'The truth is more interesting. “Soccer” was invented in England, used there for decades, and survived in North America for very practical reasons. Here is how the game came to have two English names, why each stuck where it did and what the sport is called in other languages around the world.'] },
      { h: 'A crowded field of footballs', p: [
        'In the mid-nineteenth century, “football” in Britain meant many different games. Schools and clubs played by their own rules: some allowed handling the ball and running with it, others did not; some permitted hacking at an opponent’s shins, others banned it. Matches between teams from different schools could begin with a long negotiation over whose rules to use.',
        'In 1863 a group of clubs met in London and formed the Football Association to agree a common code. Their rules, which prohibited carrying the ball, became known as association football. Clubs that preferred the handling game went their own way, and in 1871 they founded the Rugby Football Union. Both games were still “football”, so people needed a way to tell them apart.'] },
      { h: 'How “association” became “soccer”', p: [
        'The answer came from student slang. At Oxford University in the late nineteenth century, undergraduates had a habit of shortening words and adding “-er”: breakfast became “brekkers” and rugby football became “rugger”. Association football, abbreviated to “assoc.”, became “soccer” — sometimes spelled “socker” at first.',
        'The word spread quickly. A popular story credits an Oxford student, Charles Wreford-Brown, with the joke, though the tale is hard to verify. Whatever its exact origin, “soccer” was a thoroughly British word, used for much of the twentieth century in newspapers, books and everyday speech alongside “football”.'] },
      { h: 'Why Britain dropped it', p: [
        'Over the second half of the twentieth century, “soccer” gradually fell out of favour in Britain. Research by the sports economist Stefan Szymanski, who studied the word’s use in British publications, found that it remained common for decades before declining from around the 1980s, as it came to be seen as an Americanism — partly because of the attention the game received in the United States during the 1970s.',
        'Today most British fans and media use “football” almost exclusively, and some treat “soccer” as an import. It is a curious reversal: a word coined at an English university is now often regarded in England as foreign.'] },
      { h: 'Why North America kept it', p: [
        'In the United States and Canada, the name stuck for a simple reason: “football” was already taken. American and Canadian football both developed from rugby-style games played at universities in the late nineteenth century, and in the United States the rule changes associated with Walter Camp turned that game into the sport of downs and the line of scrimmage. By the time association football grew in popularity, “football” in North America meant the gridiron game.',
        'Calling the round-ball game “soccer” avoided endless confusion. In Quebec, French speakers also say “le soccer”, keeping “football” for the Canadian game. In Mexico, by contrast, fútbol has always meant the association game, and the gridiron version is called fútbol americano.'] },
      { h: 'Other countries with the same problem', p: [
        'The United States is not alone. In Australia, “football” can mean Australian rules football or rugby league depending on the region, and the round-ball game was long known as soccer. The national men’s team is still nicknamed the Socceroos, even though the governing body changed its name from the Australian Soccer Association to Football Federation Australia in 2005, and later to Football Australia.',
        'In Ireland, where Gaelic football is a major sport, “soccer” remains common in everyday speech. South Africa, where rugby is hugely popular, also often uses the word, and New Zealand, home of the All Blacks, uses both. In each case the choice of word tells you which other kind of football the local population already loved.'] },
      { h: 'What the rest of the world calls it', p: [
        'Most languages borrowed the English word or translated it. Spanish has fútbol, Portuguese futebol, French football and Dutch voetbal; German says Fußball, Finnish jalkapallo and Mandarin Chinese zúqiú, all meaning, in effect, foot-ball. In Arabic the game is kurat al-qadam, “the ball of the foot”, and in Indonesian it is sepak bola, “kick ball”.',
        'A few languages took a different path. Italians call the game calcio, from the verb “to kick” and from a Renaissance ball game played in Florence. Croatian uses nogomet, built from the words for foot and throwing, and Hungarian labdarúgás means “ball-kicking”. Spanish speakers sometimes use balompié, a home-grown coinage that survives in the name of the club Real Betis Balompié. Japanese, meanwhile, adopted the word soccer: the game is sakkā.'] },
      { h: 'Two vocabularies for one game', p: [
        'The difference goes beyond the name of the sport. In British English a match is played on a pitch, by players in kit and boots, and it can finish two-nil; American English is more likely to describe a game on a field, played in uniforms and cleats, ending two to nothing. A British fixture list is an American schedule, and the British manager who picks the team is usually called the coach or head coach in North America.',
        'The vocabularies have blended over time. American broadcasters often use British terms, and many North American clubs have adopted “FC” — football club — in their names, as Los Angeles FC, FC Cincinnati and Austin FC have done. Fans tend to adopt whatever their favourite broadcasters say, which is why the language of the game keeps shifting.'] },
      { h: 'Soccer’s American story', p: [
        'The game has a long history in the United States. The national federation was founded in 1913, and the American team reached the semi-finals of the first World Cup in 1930. In 1950 it produced one of the tournament’s great shocks, beating England 1–0 in Belo Horizonte. For decades, though, the sport struggled to compete with baseball, American football and basketball for attention.',
        'The North American Soccer League changed that for a while in the 1970s, when stars such as Pelé, Franz Beckenbauer and Johan Cruyff played in the United States and the New York Cosmos drew large crowds. The league collapsed in the mid-1980s, but the seed had been planted. The 1994 World Cup, hosted by the United States, set a total attendance record that still stood when the 2022 tournament ended, and a commitment made during the bid led to the launch of Major League Soccer, which began play in 1996.'] },
      { h: 'Soccer in North America today', p: [
        'The modern game in the region is far stronger than its old reputation suggests. Major League Soccer has expanded steadily since its first season, adding clubs across the United States and Canada, many of them with their own purpose-built stadiums. Canada launched its own professional competition, the Canadian Premier League, in 2019, and Mexico’s Liga MX remains one of the best-supported leagues in the Americas, followed closely by Spanish-speaking viewers in the United States as well as at home.',
        'The women’s game has done even more to make the sport mainstream. The United States women’s national team has won four World Cups, the National Women’s Soccer League began play in 2013, and Canada’s women won Olympic gold at the Tokyo Games. For millions of North Americans, the first great soccer team they ever watched was a women’s team, and the word “soccer” carries none of the doubt it once did.'] },
      { h: 'A practical note for travelling fans', p: [
        'If you travel to watch the game in North America, the word you use will rarely cause a problem, but local habits help. In the United States and English-speaking Canada, “soccer” is what you will see on bar signs, ticket sites and sports pages; in Quebec you will hear “le soccer”; and in Mexico “fútbol” is everywhere. Ask for “the football” in an American sports bar on an autumn weekend and you may well be shown the gridiron game instead.'] },
      { h: 'Does the word matter?', p: [
        'In the end, both words describe the same game, and both have deep English roots. Saying “soccer” in North America is not a mistake; it is the natural word in a place where “football” means something else. Saying “football” elsewhere is equally natural. The governing body itself, the Fédération Internationale de Football Association, keeps the word “association” in its name — the very word that gave us “soccer”.',
        'At a three-country World Cup, fans hear fútbol, football and soccer, often in the same stadium. That mix is fitting for a tournament whose whole point is to bring the world together around one game, whatever it happens to be called.'] },
      { h: 'In short', p: [
        '“Soccer” is a British invention, a piece of nineteenth-century Oxford slang for association football that was used in Britain for decades before it fell out of favour. It survived in the United States, Canada, Australia, Ireland and South Africa because another sport had already claimed the word “football”.',
        'Most of the world uses a version of “football”, a few languages have words of their own, and everyone is talking about the same game. Call it whatever you like.'] },
    ],
  },
  {
    slug: 'expected-goals-football-stats-explained',
    category: 'tactics',
    title: 'Expected Goals and Other Football Numbers, Explained Without the Jargon',
    excerpt:
      'Broadcasts now quote expected goals, possession and pressing statistics as readily as the score. What these numbers actually measure, how to read them during a World Cup, and the traps that make them so easy to misuse.',
    date: '2026-10-13',
    minutes: 9,
    author: 'The Xaa Desk',
    sections: [
      { h: '', p: [
        'Not long ago, the statistics on a football broadcast were limited to goals, shots, corners and perhaps possession. Today viewers hear about expected goals, pressing intensity, progressive passes and distance covered, often before half-time. Used well, these numbers reveal patterns the eye can miss; used carelessly, they can make a match sound like something it was not.',
        'This guide explains the most common football statistics in plain language: what each one measures, how to read it during a World Cup and the traps that catch even experienced analysts.'] },
      { h: 'What expected goals measures', p: [
        'Expected goals, usually shortened to xG, estimates how likely a shot is to be scored, based on how often similar shots have been scored in the past. Each shot is given a value between zero and one. A shot with an xG of 0.5 is one that, historically, has gone in about half the time; a shot worth 0.05 is scored roughly once in twenty attempts.',
        'Add up the values of all a team’s shots and you get its expected goals for the match. If a team scores three goals from chances worth a total of 0.8, it was clinical, fortunate or facing poor goalkeeping. If it creates chances worth 2.5 and scores none, it was wasteful, unlucky or facing an inspired goalkeeper. The number does not tell you which — but it tells you where to look.'] },
      { h: 'How a model values a shot', p: [
        'An xG model is built from a large historical database of shots. The most important factors are where the shot was taken — the distance from goal and the angle — and how: a header from a cross is harder to score than a shot with the foot from the same spot. Models also consider the type of pass that set up the chance, whether the shot followed a dribble or a rebound, and whether it came in open play, from a set piece or on a counter-attack.',
        'More advanced models add the positions of the goalkeeper and defenders at the moment of the shot. That matters: a shot from twelve yards with the goalkeeper stranded is very different from one taken through a crowd of bodies. Penalties are a special case with a fixed value; most models put a penalty at roughly three-quarters of a goal, reflecting how often penalties are converted.'] },
      { h: 'Reading a match through expected goals', p: [
        'A single total can hide a great deal, so look at how it was built. Ten long-range efforts worth 0.05 each add up to the same 0.5 as one clear chance from close range, but they describe very different matches. Shot maps, which plot where each shot was taken and how good the chance was, tell the story far better than a single figure.',
        'Timing matters too. A team that went ahead early and then defended deep will often concede more shots and more expected goals afterwards, not because it played badly but because it chose to protect its lead and invite pressure. Analysts call this game state, and it is one of the most common reasons why raw numbers mislead.'] },
      { h: 'Where expected goals misleads', p: [
        'Different providers use different models, so the same match can produce noticeably different xG figures depending on the source. Treat the numbers as estimates, not measurements. xG also counts only shots: a dangerous cross that flashes across the six-yard box untouched, or a through-ball cut out at the last moment, may have been the best opportunity of the match and still register nothing.',
        'Most importantly, xG describes probabilities, and probabilities need large samples. Over a season of league football, a team’s expected goals are a good guide to its underlying quality. Over three group matches at a World Cup, chance plays a much larger role. A team can play well and go home; another can ride its luck into the knockout rounds. The numbers help to explain what happened, but they cannot tell you that a result was wrong.'] },
      { h: 'Finishing and goalkeeping', p: [
        'Comparing goals with expected goals is a popular way to judge finishing. Over short periods the comparison is mostly noise: a striker who scores five goals from chances worth two in a tournament is on a hot streak that may or may not reflect lasting skill. Over many seasons, a small number of elite finishers do consistently score more than their expected goals, but far fewer than the headlines suggest.',
        'For goalkeepers, analysts use post-shot expected goals, which takes into account where a shot on target was placed: a shot into the top corner has a higher post-shot value than one hit straight at the goalkeeper. Comparing the post-shot value of the shots a goalkeeper faced with the goals actually conceded gives an estimate of how many goals he or she prevented — a better guide than the number of saves, which mostly reflects how many shots the defence allowed.'] },
      { h: 'Possession: the most misunderstood number', p: [
        'Possession tells you which team had the ball more, not which team played better. Providers calculate it in different ways — some from each team’s share of passes, others from the time each side spent in control of the ball — and it is heavily influenced by game state and strategy. Many successful teams are happy to concede possession and attack quickly when they win the ball back.',
        'World Cups provide plenty of examples. In 2022 Germany had most of the ball and many more shots than Japan in their group match, yet lost 2–1. Later in the same tournament, Spain dominated possession against Morocco in the round of 16 but created few clear chances, and Morocco went through on penalties on the way to a historic semi-final. Possession describes how a match was played, not who deserved to win it.'] },
      { h: 'Measuring the press', p: [
        'Pressing statistics try to capture how aggressively a team works to win the ball back. One common measure counts how many passes the opponent is allowed to make in its own part of the pitch for each defensive action — a tackle, interception, challenge or foul — by the pressing team. The lower the number, the more intense the press, because the opponent is being disturbed after only a few passes.',
        'Like possession, pressing numbers describe a choice rather than quality. A team that presses high and fails can concede chances behind its defence; a team that sits deep may allow many passes but few dangerous ones. The useful question is whether the pressing worked: whether the team won the ball in dangerous areas and turned those recoveries into chances.'] },
      { h: 'Passes, carries and line breaks', p: [
        'Pass completion is easy to measure and easy to misread. A centre-back who plays safe sideways passes can complete almost all of them; a playmaker who attempts difficult forward passes will complete fewer, yet create far more. That is why analysts increasingly count progressive passes and carries, which move the ball significantly closer to the opponent’s goal, and passes into the penalty area.',
        'FIFA has also published measures such as line breaks at recent tournaments — passes or runs with the ball that take it past one of the opposing team’s lines of players. Numbers like these reward the risk-taking that unlocks defences, and they help to identify players whose contribution does not show up in goals and assists.'] },
      { h: 'Running numbers', p: [
        'Distance covered and the number of sprints are now standard on broadcasts, thanks to tracking systems that follow every player throughout a match. They show effort and physical demand, and they are valuable to coaches planning recovery during a tournament. As a measure of quality, though, they are weak: a team chasing the ball because it cannot keep it will often run further than its opponent, and a well-positioned player may simply need to run less.',
        'Tracking data also feeds more sophisticated measures, such as how quickly a team recovers its defensive shape after losing the ball, or how much space a player creates for others with his or her runs. These are used mostly inside clubs and federations rather than on broadcasts, but they show where football analysis is heading: away from simply counting events and towards understanding the whole pitch.'] },
      { h: 'Using numbers during a World Cup', p: [
        'A few habits make statistics far more useful. Compare each figure with the opponent’s, because a number in isolation means little. Look at chance quality rather than totals: big chances, shot maps and expected goals per shot. Adjust for game state, especially after an early goal or a red card. For players, prefer rates per ninety minutes to raw totals, so that a substitute is not compared directly with someone who played every minute.',
        'Above all, remember how small a tournament sample is. Three group matches are not enough to rank teams with confidence, and a knockout tie is a single match decided by fine margins. Numbers are excellent at raising questions — why did this team concede so many chances, why did that striker get so few shots — and much less reliable at providing final answers.'] },
      { h: 'In short', p: [
        'Expected goals measures the quality of chances, based on how often similar shots have been scored. It is most useful over many matches and should be read alongside shot maps and game state. Possession, pressing and running numbers describe how a team chose to play, not whether it played well.',
        'Use statistics as a companion to watching, not a substitute for it. The best analysts combine the two: the numbers tell them where to look, and their eyes tell them what really happened.'] },
    ],
  },
];
